A||||11||Standard||I need a heated garage...||||14||
Z||000000||Standard||02-16-2000||12:25 AM||||or a bigger space heater.  Swapped the front brake rotors from my 84 to my 86.  Why would I do this?  Because the rotors on my GT were pretty grooved,  checker auto didn't have any new one's in stock (they wanted 49.99 each anyways), and the one's on the 84 are in really good shape.  also did new brake pads and new inner and outer wheel bearings.<P>Took about 4 hours, from rolling both cars into the garage to getting them both back on the ground.  I found it fairly easy, this was my first time ever actually taking off the rotor and doing wheel bearing work.  <P>The only minor annoyance was the pretty tight clearance on the lower caliper mounting bolt for the 86.  Lower control arm design is different from 86 to 84, the 86 is wider towards the end.  Interesting to actually see the differences.  If i had a digital camera i would take some pics.. but i don't...<BR>1 1/2" pvc pipe works pretty good for pounding in the inner bearing race.  Couldn't find a socket big enough so I had to sacrifice the barrel of one of my spud guns..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <P>I was amazed at how rust-free my GT is!  I was afraid it might be rusted to bits, but I got a light under there and was surprized.. the only things that have any amount of rust are the subframe connecters, and the front of the coolant pipes are a little spotty.  Looks like it's just surface rust, tho.  <P>I do have a question after all this... before and after I did the wheel bearing on the 86, there is some vertical play when the tires are off the ground.  Does this mean the balljoints are going?  I wouldn't be surprized.. the whole front end of the car looks original.  for everybody who made it to the bottom, thanks!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000001||2M4 Dale||02-17-2000||12:24 AM||||If you have seated the wheel bearings correctly and there is still vertical movement then I'd say you have a ball joint problem. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||02-17-2000||12:58 AM||||I'm pretty sure I did it right.  Torque to 12 ft/lbs, loosen to just loose, then finger tight, line up with hole.  i'm pretty good with bearings, rebuilt plenty of bike hubs and headsets when i was a mechanic..<P>I'm gonna take it in to the Tire's plus that my friends dad manages and have them look at it..||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||02-17-2000||01:01 AM||||Well, there's supposed to be a little play, because of the way you hand tighten the wheel nut. Do we have hard technical data out there from anyone?  ||||reg||1
